 ## Background
-The need for surface geometry constraints in 3D Gaussian Splatting arises from the method's inherent volumetric nature, which can lead to "floaters" and ambiguous surface boundaries when reconstructing sharp geometric features [S2]. Early implementations relied on manual collision geometry to provide physical boundaries for navigation, as the splats themselves did not constitute solid surfaces [S3, unofficial]. Subsequent research introduced explicit constraints, such as planar alignment using foundation model priors [S1], mesh-adherent scale bounding [S1], and tangent plane parameterization for normal estimation [S2]. More recent approaches utilize spherical shell constraints for outdoor background reconstruction to prevent divergence [S4]. These methods collectively aim to bridge the gap between differentiable volumetric rendering and explicit geometric surface representation.
